ndependent lightweight check: reconstruct the spot from a random sample of reference cells (digital mixture simulation) — for example, test how well k-cell pools from various combinations fit. Or, more simply: compute the correlation between the spot and a centroid mixture: 0.32*B + 0.32*Mac + 0.35*Endo, and compare to the pure endothelial centroid.\n\nAlso, let me check the neighbors: the neighbor average is Fibroblast 0.50, T_Cell 0.31, Endothelial 0.12 — spatially this spot is in a region different from its neighbors? Strange: this spot itself is B/Mac/Endo while the neighbors are Fibro/T cell. Hmm, this suggests a sharp spatial domain boundary, which is plausible for a simulated tissue with distinct regions (e.g., an immune/B-cell zone adjacent to stroma/T-cell zones).\n\nActually, let me reconsider: NNLS on CPM without any complexity adjustment can bias weights. The simulated data is probably generated as a sum of cells of different types. A spot total of 3693 UMIs vs about 2000 per cell suggests the spot might contain about 2 cells' worth of RNA (Visium spots capture multiple cells). \n\nLet me do one more targeted diagnostic (allowed: at most one targeted follow-up if answers conflict). Conflict: nearest-neighbor voting says nearly pure endothelial; NNLS says a 3-way mixture. Targeted check:\n- Compare Pearson r of the spot against the pure endothelial centroid vs. against the NNLS mixture reconstruction.\n- Check per-cell correlations: is there a gap between the top correlations? What are the types of cells ranked around 20–40?\n- Do a simple grid search over mixtures: does the fit R² improve substantially with a mixture compared to the best single type?\n\nLet me run a single diagnostic script."}}
